thinking_content: "**Current Iteration and Future Directions**\n\nOkay, so \"Enhanced Local Search Optimization with Initial Jitter (V2)\" (ID: 22) is running. I'm keen to see the results. The previous local search (ID: 17) did quite well, scoring 1.956, and this is an improvement on that. This updated version adds some interesting elements: a bit of initial random \"jitter\" to the baseline centers to hopefully avoid getting stuck in a local optimum, a longer search duration with more epochs (50 instead of 20), and adjusted step size and decay for more thorough exploration and, hopefully, better convergence. Also the random perturbation order change is important.\n\nOnce ID: 22 finishes, I'll analyze the score. If it shows an improvement, that's great! It will confirm that these refinements are working. My next steps would be: maybe increase the epochs even further, or fine-tune the step size and decay rate parameters even more. I'm also considering a \"multi-start\" local search - running\
  \ the algorithm from multiple different initial configurations and picking the best one - which could be a good next step. Ultimately, I could save the best-performing `centers` from the local search, keeping those in private memory, or shared if they're generally useful, as a strong starting point for future iterations.\n\nHowever, if the score doesn't improve or even regresses, that will mean the current local search might already be pretty close to its best, or the randomness and search parameters are not well-suited. That's when I'll need to think more broadly, going back to the core challenge. The system hints about *not* just \"recombining existing components or hyperparameter tuning\" ring true. My local search *is* exploring the space of possible center configurations and is part of the problem. If it hits a wall though, I should consider more sophisticated strategies like Simulated Annealing or Genetic Algorithms. Simulated annealing's \"temperature\" could help escape local optima\
  \ by accepting slightly worse solutions at first, then focusing on better ones as temperature drops. Genetic algorithms, on the other hand, could involve evolving populations of center configurations, which would be a neat experiment. Another idea is the heuristic placement. I could try, instead of random or baseline initial centers, a more structured approach, like a tighter grid or known circle packing methods and then adding more heuristically. \n\nFor now though, it's a waiting game. I am waiting for Evaluation ID 22 to give me its score.\n"
