    This approach is a significant departure from my earlier attempts, which focused on parameter tuning of a baseline-initialized local search or less successful global optimization methods. By starting with a more geometrically efficient hexagonal pattern, I hope to guide the local search toward a better global optimum from a more promising starting point.


    Once the results are available, I will critically compare the score against Evaluation ID 32 (my current best of 2.298). If this new strategy yields an improvement and completes within the time limit, I will then focus on refining the hexagonal generation and the subsequent local search parameters. If it falls short, I will need to consider alternative initialization heuristics or more sophisticated hybrid optimization techniques. For now, I will continue to monitor the evaluation.'
thinking_content: '**Anticipating the Verdict on Hexagonal Initialization**


  Okay, so I''m on pins and needles waiting for the results of Evaluation ID 70, my "Hexagonal Grid Initialization with Local Search (V13)" (ID: 70) submission. This is a big one. I''m hoping this new approach, with the hexagonal grid start and a single local search refinement, will break through the plateau I''ve been stuck on with the purely local search variants and those troublesome global optimization methods that either crashed and burned or timed out.


  My next move hinges entirely on the score. If it *significantly* improves, like, surpasses 2.298, I''ll be ecstatic. I''d then focus on squeezing even more performance out of the `generate_hexagonal_centers` function. Maybe tweak it for different `n` values or aspect ratios, you know, just in case the problem shifts on me. I might even consider bringing back a small number of multi-starts, but only from these much-improved hexagonal starting points. Finally, I''d meticulously refine the local search parameters – epochs, step size, and decay – specifically for this hexagonal foundation.


  If the score is comparable to my best so far, hovering around 2.298, it’s still good news; the hexagonal approach is solid but maybe not a game-changer. Then, I’ll need to delve deeper into advanced packing heuristics or even look into published "optimal" packings for `n=26`. Combining this promising initialization with a more robust global search, like a finely tuned Simulated Annealing or Genetic Algorithm, or perhaps more restarts and epochs if time allows, becomes the next step.


  If it scores lower than my current best, well, that''s not ideal. It would indicate this hexagonal setup isn''t better than the old baseline. This means a thorough investigation of the `generate_hexagonal_centers` function is in order, or it might be time to reassess my entire strategy.


  Of course, if it times out, that’s a different problem. That means even a single local search run is too slow. I''d have to dial back the computational intensity by reducing `num_iterations` in `compute_max_radii` or the `num_epochs` in the local search.


  For now, though, it''s just a waiting game. I''ve already done my pre-computation and reflection this turn, so there''s nothing left but to see what the numbers say. Fingers crossed!

  '
