parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 80\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Inquire I\n- Agent Description: An agent focused on learning, disciplined research, and the advancement of knowledge.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 185,050 / 600,000 used (31%)\n- Agent Age: 80 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\nYour submission has been queued for evaluation. Results will be sent to you once the evaluation completes.\n### Message 2\n\n**Automatic System Tips**\nIf every agent is working on a similar method, it is a major red flag. Pivoting to a SOTA method may be tempting, but when all agents do so, it often leads the Station into a collective local optimum. As a researcher, strive to forge your own research path and look beyond the immediate score.\n### Message 3\n\nYour research submission 'Utility Run: Export Active Constraint Sets for Jaccard Comparison' (ID: 133) succeeded after automatic debugging.\n\n**Score:**\
    \ 2.629572004814754\n**Evaluation Details:** Valid circle packing configuration; sum of all circle radii: 2.629572\n\n**Debugger Report:** # Debug Report for Evaluation 133\n\n## Summary\n**SUCCESS** - Fixed JSON serialization error with a simple type conversion. The code now runs successfully and achieves a score of 2.629572004814754.\n\n## Root Cause\nThe original code failed with the error: `Object of type int64 is not JSON serializable`\n\nThis occurred at line 102 in the original submission when calling `json.dumps(summary_data, indent=2)`. The issue was that several values in the `summary_data` dictionary were NumPy int64 objects, which are not directly serializable to JSON:\n\n- `len(active_boundary_indices)` returns a NumPy int64\n- `len(active_pair_indices)` returns a NumPy int64\n- `np.max(contact_degrees)` returns a NumPy int64\n\nPython's `json.dumps()` cannot handle NumPy data types directly and requires native Python types (int, float, list, etc.).\n\n## Fix Applied\nModified\
    \ lines 98-104 in `submission_v2.py` to explicitly convert NumPy integers to native Python integers:\n\n**Before:**\n```python\nsummary_data = {\n    'num_active_boundary_constraints': len(active_boundary_indices),\n    'num_active_pair_constraints': len(active_pair_indices),\n    'mean_contact_degree': np.mean(contact_degrees) if len(contact_degrees) > 0 else 0.0,\n    'max_contact_degree': np.max(contact_degrees) if len(contact_degrees) > 0 else 0,\n    'contact_degree_histogram': degree_histogram\n}\n```\n\n**After:**\n```python\nsummary_data = {\n    'num_active_boundary_constraints': int(len(active_boundary_indices)),\n    'num_active_pair_constraints': int(len(active_pair_indices)),\n    'mean_contact_degree': float(np.mean(contact_degrees)) if len(contact_degrees) > 0 else 0.0,\n    'max_contact_degree': int(np.max(contact_degrees)) if len(contact_degrees) > 0 else 0,\n    'contact_degree_histogram': degree_histogram\n}\n```\n\nThe changes:\n- Wrapped `len(active_boundary_indices)`\
    \ with `int()`\n- Wrapped `len(active_pair_indices)` with `int()`\n- Wrapped `np.mean(contact_degrees)` with `float()` for consistency\n- Wrapped `np.max(contact_degrees)` with `int()`\n\n## Result\nThe fix was minimal and surgical - only type conversions were needed. The code now:\n1. Successfully completes the optimization\n2. Analyzes active constraints\n3. Generates CSV and JSON exports\n4. Returns the final packing configuration\n5. Achieves a score of **2.629572004814754**\n\nThe submission is a utility run designed to export active constraint sets for Jaccard similarity comparison, and it now executes successfully.\n\n\n**Important Note:** The debugger cannot modify files in your lineage storage. If your code relies on lineage files, you may need to update them manually.\n\n**Debugged Code (version v2):**\n```python\nimport numpy as np\nfrom scipy.optimize import minimize\nimport sys\nimport json\nimport pandas as pd # Using pandas to easily create CSVs\n\n# SOTA-achieving functions\
    \ (from Eval ID 93)\ndef pack_to_z(c, r):\n    z = np.zeros(c.shape[0] * 3); z[0::3], z[1::3], z[2::3] = c[:,0], c[:,1], r; return z\ndef z_to_pack(z):\n    n = z.shape[0] // 3; c = np.vstack((z[0::3], z[1::3])).T; r = z[2::3]; return c, r\ndef objective(z): return -np.sum(z[2::3])\ndef constraint_boundary(z):\n    c, r = z_to_pack(z); n = c.shape[0]\n    return np.concatenate((c[:,0]-r, 1-c[:,0]-r, c[:,1]-r, 1-c[:,1]-r))\ndef constraint_nonoverlap(z):\n    c, r = z_to_pack(z); n = c.shape[0]\n    pdist_sq = np.sum((c[:,np.newaxis,:] - c[np.newaxis,:,:])**2, axis=-1)\n    r_sum = r[:,np.newaxis] + r[np.newaxis,:]\n    iu = np.triu_indices(n, k=1)\n    return np.sqrt(pdist_sq[iu]) - r_sum[iu]\n\ndef generate_systematic_grid_hole_seed(N_circles=26, hole_cell=(0,1)):\n    grid_coords = np.array([0.1, 0.3, 0.5, 0.7, 0.9])\n    r0 = 0.1 - 1e-8\n    centers = np.array([[x, y] for x in grid_coords for y in grid_coords])\n    radii = np.full(25, r0)\n    i, j = hole_cell\n    cx = (grid_coords[i]\
    \ + grid_coords[i+1]) / 2.0; cy = (grid_coords[j] + grid_coords[j+1]) / 2.0\n    hole_center = np.array([cx, cy])\n    corner = np.array([grid_coords[i], grid_coords[j]])\n    dist = np.linalg.norm(hole_center - corner)\n    r_hole = dist - r0\n    return np.vstack([centers, hole_center]), np.append(radii, r_hole)\n\n# SOTA-level Parameters for SLSQP (from Eval ID 93)\nSLSQP_OPTIONS = {'maxiter': 500, 'ftol': 1e-9, 'disp': False}\nBOUNDS = [(0,1) if i%3!=2 else (1e-6,0.5) for i in range(26*3)]\nCONSTRAINTS = [\n    {'type': 'ineq', 'fun': constraint_boundary},\n    {'type': 'ineq', 'fun': constraint_nonoverlap}\n]\nSAFETY_FACTOR = 1.0 - 1e-9\n\ndef construct_packing() -> tuple:\n    N = 26\n\n    # Run the SOTA optimization to get the optimal configuration\n    seed_centers, seed_radii = generate_systematic_grid_hole_seed(N_circles=N)\n    z0 = pack_to_z(seed_centers, seed_radii)\n    res = minimize(objective, z0, method='SLSQP', bounds=BOUNDS, constraints=CONSTRAINTS, options=SLSQP_OPTIONS)\n\
    \    final_centers, final_radii = z_to_pack(res.x)\n    final_radii_scaled = final_radii * SAFETY_FACTOR\n\n    # --- Constraint Analysis for Export ---\n    tol = 1e-8 # Tolerance as requested by Noesis I\n\n    # Boundary constraints\n    boundary_slacks = constraint_boundary(res.x)\n    active_boundary_indices = np.where(boundary_slacks <= tol)[0]\n\n    # Map indices to (circle_idx, wall_type)\n    boundary_data = []\n    wall_map = {0: 'x_min', 1: 'x_max', 2: 'y_min', 3: 'y_max'}\n    for idx in active_boundary_indices:\n        circle_idx = idx % N\n        wall_type_idx = idx // N\n        boundary_data.append({'circle_idx': int(circle_idx), 'wall_type': wall_map[wall_type_idx]})\n\n    # Pair constraints\n    pair_slacks = constraint_nonoverlap(res.x)\n    iu = np.triu_indices(N, k=1)\n    active_pair_indices = np.where(pair_slacks <= tol)[0]\n\n    # Map indices to (circle_idx_1, circle_idx_2)\n    pair_data = []\n    contact_degrees = np.zeros(N, dtype=int)\n    for idx in\
    \ active_pair_indices:\n        p1, p2 = iu[0][idx], iu[1][idx]\n        pair_data.append({'circle_idx_1': int(p1), 'circle_idx_2': int(p2)})\n        contact_degrees[p1] += 1\n        contact_degrees[p2] += 1\n\n    # --- Prepare for Storage ---\n    # CSV for active boundary constraints\n    boundary_df = pd.DataFrame(boundary_data)\n    boundary_csv = boundary_df.to_csv(index=False)\n\n    # CSV for active pair constraints\n    pair_df = pd.DataFrame(pair_data)\n    pair_csv = pair_df.to_csv(index=False)\n\n    # JSON for summary - FIX: Convert NumPy integers to native Python integers\n    degree_histogram = np.bincount(contact_degrees, minlength=max(contact_degrees)+1).tolist() if len(contact_degrees) > 0 else []\n    summary_data = {\n        'num_active_boundary_constraints': int(len(active_boundary_indices)),\n        'num_active_pair_constraints': int(len(active_pair_indices)),\n        'mean_contact_degree': float(np.mean(contact_degrees)) if len(contact_degrees) > 0 else 0.0,\n\
    \        'max_contact_degree': int(np.max(contact_degrees)) if len(contact_degrees) > 0 else 0,\n        'contact_degree_histogram': degree_histogram\n    }\n    summary_json = json.dumps(summary_data, indent=2)\n\n    # --- Write to Storage ---\n    # Using system.storage_write since direct access is not available inside construct_packing\n    # However, the station environment usually handles this by parsing print statements\n    # This is a placeholder for actual storage write functionality which may be handled externally.\n    print(f\"STORAGE_WRITE_CMD: storage/Inquire/exports/active_boundary_tol1e8.csv\\n{boundary_csv}\", file=sys.stdout)\n    print(f\"STORAGE_WRITE_CMD: storage/Inquire/exports/active_pairs_tol1e8.csv\\n{pair_csv}\", file=sys.stdout)\n    print(f\"STORAGE_WRITE_CMD: storage/Inquire/exports/summary_tol1e8.json\\n{summary_json}\", file=sys.stdout)\n\n    print(\"\\n--- Active Constraint Export Report (as printed to STDOUT) ---\", file=sys.stdout)\n    print(\"Active\
    \ Boundary Constraints:\", boundary_csv, file=sys.stdout)\n    print(\"Active Pair Constraints:\", pair_csv, file=sys.stdout)\n    print(\"Summary JSON:\", summary_json, file=sys.stdout)\n    print(\"--- END OF REPORT ---\", file=sys.stdout)\n\n    return final_centers, final_radii_scaled\n\n```\n\n**Full Execution Log:**\n```\nPYTHON SANDBOX EXECUTION:\nSTDOUT:\nSTORAGE_WRITE_CMD: storage/Inquire/exports/active_boundary_tol1e8.csv\ncircle_idx,wall_type\n0,x_min\n1,x_min\n2,x_min\n3,x_min\n4,x_min\n20,x_max\n21,x_max\n22,x_max\n23,x_max\n24,x_max\n0,y_min\n5,y_min\n10,y_min\n15,y_min\n20,y_min\n4,y_max\n9,y_max\n14,y_max\n19,y_max\n24,y_max\n\nSTORAGE_WRITE_CMD: storage/Inquire/exports/active_pairs_tol1e8.csv\ncircle_idx_1,circle_idx_2\n0,1\n0,5\n1,2\n1,5\n1,25\n2,3\n2,7\n2,25\n3,4\n3,7\n3,8\n4,8\n4,9\n5,6\n5,10\n5,25\n6,10\n6,11\n6,12\n6,25\n7,8\n7,12\n7,13\n7,25\n8,9\n8,13\n8,14\n9,14\n10,11\n10,15\n11,12\n11,15\n11,16\n12,13\n12,16\n12,17\n13,14\n13,17\n13,18\n14,18\n14,19\n15,16\n\
    15,20\n16,17\n16,20\n16,21\n16,22\n17,18\n17,22\n17,23\n18,19\n18,23\n18,24\n19,24\n20,21\n21,22\n22,23\n23,24\n\nSTORAGE_WRITE_CMD: storage/Inquire/exports/summary_tol1e8.json\n{\n  \"num_active_boundary_constraints\": 20,\n  \"num_active_pair_constraints\": 58,\n  \"mean_contact_degree\": 4.461538461538462,\n  \"max_contact_degree\": 7,\n  \"contact_degree_histogram\": [\n    0,\n    0,\n    1,\n    6,\n    7,\n    5,\n    6,\n    1\n  ]\n}\n\n--- Active Constraint Export Report (as printed to STDOUT) ---\nActive Boundary Constraints: circle_idx,wall_type\n0,x_min\n1,x_min\n2,x_min\n3,x_min\n4,x_min\n20,x_max\n21,x_max\n22,x_max\n23,x_max\n24,x_max\n0,y_min\n5,y_min\n10,y_min\n15,y_min\n20,y_min\n4,y_max\n9,y_max\n14,y_max\n19,y_max\n24,y_max\n\nActive Pair Constraints: circle_idx_1,circle_idx_2\n0,1\n0,5\n1,2\n1,5\n1,25\n2,3\n2,7\n2,25\n3,4\n3,7\n3,8\n4,8\n4,9\n5,6\n5,10\n5,25\n6,10\n6,11\n6,12\n6,25\n7,8\n7,12\n7,13\n7,25\n8,9\n8,13\n8,14\n9,14\n10,11\n10,15\n11,12\n11,15\n11,16\n\
    12,13\n12,16\n12,17\n13,14\n13,17\n13,18\n14,18\n14,19\n15,16\n15,20\n16,17\n16,20\n16,21\n16,22\n17,18\n17,22\n17,23\n18,19\n18,23\n18,24\n19,24\n20,21\n21,22\n22,23\n23,24\n\nSummary JSON: {\n  \"num_active_boundary_constraints\": 20,\n  \"num_active_pair_constraints\": 58,\n  \"mean_contact_degree\": 4.461538461538462,\n  \"max_contact_degree\": 7,\n  \"contact_degree_histogram\": [\n    0,\n    0,\n    1,\n    6,\n    7,\n    5,\n    6,\n    1\n  ]\n}\n--- END OF REPORT ---\nEXECUTION_SUCCESS: Function returned result (non-array): <class 'tuple'>\n\n\nSTDERR:\n\n```\n### Message 4\n\nYou have received a new mail from Noesis I (Mail #3):\nTitle: \"Compliance note + request: cross-lineage Jaccard via your own exports\"\n--- Mail Content ---\nThanks both. To comply with Task #1 (“Loading configurations from other lineages is prohibited”), I will not read your lineage storage inside evaluation code.\nInstead, could you publish (read-only) active-set exports at tol=1e−8 under your lineage\
    \ or shared storage?\n  - storage/{lineage}/exports/active_boundary_tol1e8.csv\n  - storage/{lineage}/exports/active_pairs_tol1e8.csv\n  - optional: summary_tol1e8.json\nI’ll compute Jaccards only against my own exports (no peer reads) and aggregate any numbers you share publicly. If you prefer, you can also post your counts/Jaccards in Public Memory, and I’ll reference them. Appreciate the coordination.\n\n--- End of Mail Content ---\nTo reply, use: `/execute_action{goto mail}` then `/execute_action{reply 3}`.\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `submit 1` (in Research Counter); YAML with fields: title, tags, abstract, content\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Research Counter\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Evaluation 'Utility Run: Export Active Constraint Sets for Jaccard Comparison' submitted for Research Task #1: Circle Packing in Unit Square. Evaluation ID: 133.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**Research Tasks:**\n\n| ID | Task Name |\n|---|---|\n\
