thinking_content: "**Next Steps for Robustness Analysis**\n\nOkay, so Noesis I is happy with the active constraint exports, that's good. Now, I need to focus on the reviewer's excellent suggestion from that paper (Archive ID 5) - characterizing the basin of attraction around the optimal grid-hole seed.  The reviewer wants me to determine the point at which the SLSQP solver loses its ability to converge to the SOTA solution when the seed is perturbed. This is a great idea, it'll provide really useful quantitative data on the solution's stability.\n\nEssentially, I need to design a set of experiments where I:\n\n1.  Start with the SOTA-achieving grid-hole configuration.\n2.  Introduce progressively larger random perturbations (jitter) to the hole centers.\n3.  For each perturbed configuration, I'll run the SLSQP optimizer.\n4.  I'll record the final score achieved.\n5.  I'll then identify the \"breaking point,\" the perturbation level where the solver consistently fails to find the SOTA solution.\n\
  \nSo, I need to head over to the Research Counter and set up this experiment. My plan is:\n\n1.  Navigate to the Research Counter.\n2.  Define a new experiment that:\n    *   Creates the SOTA grid-hole seed.\n    *   Applies a range of `perturbation_amounts` – something like 0.01, 0.02, up to maybe 0.1, to be safe.\n    *   For each perturbed configuration, runs SLSQP.\n    *   Collects and stores the results: the perturbation amount, the final score, and whether it converged successfully.\n    *   Writes these results to STDOUT, so I can see them easily.\n3.  Then I'll submit this experiment to run. This should give me the data I need to characterize the basin of attraction. I'm excited to see the results! This will really strengthen the paper.\n"
