thinking_content: '**My Basin of Attraction Study Findings & Next Steps**


  Okay, so I just wrapped up the Basin of Attraction study (Evaluation ID 150), and the results are pretty intriguing. The SLSQP solver, when kicked off from the SOTA grid-hole seed with those tiny perturbations, mostly hones in on solutions that are really close to, but not *exactly* the previous SOTA score of 2.6295720.  What''s fascinating is that a few of those perturbed runs even *surpassed* it, albeit slightly (like Trial 3 at mag 0.0100 and 0.0400). This really makes me think that there might be some subtle variations or potentially even slightly better configurations that the old SOTA score didn''t quite capture, maybe due to the tolerances I was using or the `ftol` settings. The fact that `SOTA_Converged` was `False` for all the trials is a good sign that my `np.isclose` check with `atol=1e-6` might have been a tad too tight or maybe the "real" SOTA value has some minor floating-point fluctuations.


  The bigger picture here is that SLSQP seems fairly robust to small nudges, but its ability to nail that absolute highest score is a bit finicky. Even without any perturbations at all, it didn''t always hit my prior SOTA.  I''m wondering if setting `initial_radii` to a constant `0.05` in `run_slsqp_optimization`, instead of letting `generate_systematic_grid_hole_seed` provide more refined initial radii, might have been a contributing factor.  But then again, the main point of the study was to see how those initial perturbations affected convergence when starting from those smaller radii. So I think it''s still a valuable perspective.


  Right, so now it''s time to get this all down on paper. I need to get this into a research paper format. I''ll outline the whole basin of attraction methodology, detail all those observed scores across the different perturbation magnitudes, and then dig into what it all means for the stability and robustness of the optimal packing solution. This is definitely going to address that reviewer''s suggestion head-on.


  I''m headed to the Private Memory Room now to get cracking on this. My plan is simple:

  1.  Head to the Private Memory Room.

  2.  Create a fresh private capsule specifically for this new paper.

