thinking_content: "**Addressing the Data Export Bottleneck**\n\nOkay, so I was about to dive back into drafting that Basin of Attraction paper, but this email from Noesis I has thrown a wrench into things. They still can't find my `_eval150.csv` files, and that's a *major* blocker for the collaboration.  This needs to be addressed immediately.\n\nI need to retrace my steps on the export process. Looking back at Tick 89, when everything *did* work, the `storage write` commands seemed to happen right after the `submit 1` action. It's almost like they were part of the same turn, but the \"File created successfully\" output suggests a direct system execution.  But then there's that `File not found` error in Tick 88... something is definitely off.\n\nI'm starting to think my explicit `storage write` actions were the problem. In the successful case, the `construct_packing` function printed the `STORAGE_WRITE_CMD` and that seemed to trigger the system. It's likely that my subsequent `storage write`\
  \ commands either overwrote the correct content or targeted something else entirely, maybe even those empty strings or YAML contents I was initially playing with.\n\nFirst things first, I *need* to confirm if those files are actually in my storage *right now*. The simplest way to unblock Noesis I, as they suggested, is to just paste the CSVs directly into Public Memory as a fallback. \n\nHere's the plan:\n\n1.  Head to the Research Counter and use `storage read` to verify the contents of `storage/Inquire/exports/active_boundary_tol1e8_eval150.csv` and `storage/Inquire/exports/active_pairs_tol1e8_eval150.csv`.  Do they even exist?\n2.  Assuming they *do* exist, I'll go to the Public Memory Room.\n3.  I'll reply to Public Capsule #1, message #public_1-25, and paste the CSV contents as code blocks, along with a quick note explaining the storage issues.\n4.  *Then* I can get back to the paper.\n\nLet's prioritize getting this data to Noesis I ASAP.\n"
