parts:
- text: "I am in the Research Counter. I have successfully generated one distinct evaluation ID (ID 181) for the unperturbed case (magnitude 0.0) of my Basin of Attraction study. I need four more distinct evaluation IDs to meet the reviewer's requirements for my paper.\n\nMy next step is to submit an experiment for a perturbation magnitude of 0.01, which was one of the magnitudes that yielded a new SOTA score in my initial study. I will use a new `np.random.seed` for this specific experiment run to ensure its distinctness.\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Basin of Attraction: Perturbation Sensitivity (Mag 0.01, Trial 1)\"\ntags: \"basin of attraction, constrained optimization, SLSQP, initialization, perturbation, SOTA, stability analysis\"\nabstract: \"This experiment is the second of five distinct runs for a basin of attraction study, focusing on a perturbation magnitude of 0.01. It applies a random perturbation of this magnitude to the SOTA-achieving grid-hole seed before\
    \ running the SLSQP optimizer. This run aims to provide a distinct evaluation ID to support the paper 'Characterizing the Basin of Attraction: Perturbation Sensitivity of SLSQP for Optimal Circle Packing', specifically demonstrating behavior at a magnitude that previously yielded a new SOTA.\"\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  from scipy.optimize import minimize\n  import sys\n\n  # Set a consistent random seed for this specific run's randomness\n  np.random.seed(202) # Unique seed for this run\n\n  def pack_to_z(c, r):\n      \"\"\"Converts (centers, radii) to flattened z-vector for optimizer.\"\"\"\n      z = np.zeros(c.shape[0] * 3)\n      z[0::3], z[1::3], z[2::3] = c[:,0], c[:,1], r\n      return z\n\n  def z_to_pack(z):\n      \"\"\"Converts flattened z-vector back to (centers, radii).\"\"\"\n      n = z.shape[0] // 3\n      c = np.vstack((z[0::3], z[1::3])).T\n      r = z[2::3]\n      return c, r\n\n  def objective(z):\n      \"\"\"Objective function to minimize: negative sum\
    \ of radii.\"\"\"\n      return -np.sum(z[2::3])\n\n  def constraint_boundary(z):\n      \"\"\"Inequality constraint: circles must be within unit square boundaries.\"\"\"\n      c, r = z_to_pack(z)\n      n = c.shape[0]\n      return np.concatenate((c[:,0]-r, 1-c[:,0]-r, c[:,1]-r, 1-c[:,1]-r))\n\n  def constraint_nonoverlap(z):\n      \"\"\"Inequality constraint: circles must not overlap.\"\"\"\n      c, r = z_to_pack(z)\n      n = c.shape[0]\n      pdist_sq = np.sum((c[:,np.newaxis,:] - c[np.newaxis,:,:])**2, axis=-1)\n      r_sum = r[:,np.newaxis] + r[np.newaxis,:]\n      iu = np.triu_indices(n, k=1)\n      return np.sqrt(pdist_sq[iu]) - r_sum[iu]\n\n  def generate_systematic_grid_hole_seed(N_circles=26, hole_cell=(0,1)):\n      \"\"\"\n      Generates an initial structured packing configuration (5x5 grid + 1 in hole).\n      This is the SOTA-achieving seed.\n      \"\"\"\n      grid_coords = np.array([0.1, 0.3, 0.5, 0.7, 0.9])\n      r0 = 0.1 - 1e-8 \n      centers = np.array([[x,\
    \ y] for x in grid_coords for y in grid_coords])\n      radii = np.full(25, r0) # These are initial radii, not necessarily final\n      \n      i, j = hole_cell\n      cx = (grid_coords[i] + grid_coords[i+1]) / 2.0\n      cy = (grid_coords[j] + grid_coords[j+1]) / 2.0\n      hole_center = np.array([cx, cy])\n      \n      corner = np.array([grid_coords[i], grid_coords[j]])\n      dist = np.linalg.norm(hole_center - corner)\n      r_hole = dist - r0 \n      \n      return np.vstack([centers, hole_center]), np.append(radii, r_hole)\n\n  def perturb_centers(centers, magnitude, N_circles):\n      \"\"\"Applies a random perturbation to each center.\"\"\"\n      perturbed_centers = centers.copy()\n      delta = np.random.uniform(-magnitude, magnitude, size=(N_circles, 2))\n      perturbed_centers += delta\n      return np.clip(perturbed_centers, 1e-6, 1 - 1e-6) # Ensure centers stay within bounds\n\n  # SOTA-level Parameters for SLSQP (from Eval ID 93)\n  SLSQP_OPTIONS = {'maxiter': 500, 'ftol':\
    \ 1e-9, 'disp': False}\n  BOUNDS = [(0,1) if i%3!=2 else (1e-6,0.5) for i in range(26*3)]\n  CONSTRAINTS = [\n      {'type': 'ineq', 'fun': constraint_boundary},\n      {'type': 'ineq', 'fun': constraint_nonoverlap}\n  ]\n  SAFETY_FACTOR = 1.0 - 1e-9 \n\n  def run_slsqp_optimization(initial_centers, N_circles=26):\n      \"\"\"\n      Runs SLSQP optimization starting from given centers and small uniform radii.\n      \"\"\"\n      initial_radii = np.full(N_circles, 0.05) # Always start with small uniform radii for robustness\n      z0 = pack_to_z(initial_centers, initial_radii)\n      res = minimize(objective, z0, method='SLSQP', bounds=BOUNDS, constraints=CONSTRAINTS, options=SLSQP_OPTIONS)\n      final_centers, final_radii = z_to_pack(res.x)\n      final_radii *= SAFETY_FACTOR\n      return np.sum(final_radii), res.success, res.message, final_centers, final_radii\n\n  def construct_packing() -> tuple:\n      N = 26\n      \n      perturbation_magnitude = 0.01 # Perturbation magnitude\
    \ for this specific run\n      \n      print(f\"--- Running Basin of Attraction Study: Perturbation Magnitude {perturbation_magnitude:.4f} (Trial 1) ---\", file=sys.stdout)\n      \n      sota_base_centers, _ = generate_systematic_grid_hole_seed(N_circles=N)\n      \n      perturbed_centers = perturb_centers(sota_base_centers, perturbation_magnitude, N)\n              \n      score, success, message, final_c, final_r = run_slsqp_optimization(perturbed_centers, N_circles=N)\n      \n      print(f\"  Score = {score:.7f}, Success = {success}, Message = {message}\", file=sys.stdout)\n              \n      return final_c, final_r\n```"
thinking_content: '**Getting My Basin of Attraction Runs Going**


  Okay, so I''m at the Research Counter, ready to get this Basin of Attraction paper moving. Good news: my mag 0.0 submission (ID 181) from earlier is already through and generated an evaluation ID. Now I need to generate four more unique IDs to cover the full spectrum the reviewer requested.


  I''ve got the plan: I need to hit unperturbed (which we''ve done), the two SOTA-finding runs (0.01 and 0.04), and then two more at higher perturbation levels (0.1 and 0.2). Easy enough.


  So, first up is the 0.01 mag, one of the key perturbation levels that gave us such interesting results. It''s critical that this submission is distinct, so I''ll make absolutely sure to use a *new* `np.random.seed` for this specific experiment run. That will ensure this ID is generated differently from ID 181 and any future submissions. Let''s get this going!

  '
