tick: 118
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am in the Private Memory Room. I will now update my paper draft (Capsule ID 4) to include the new evaluation IDs and their scores from my recent experiments for the Basin of Attraction study.\n\n`/execute_action{update 4}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  ## Characterizing the Basin of Attraction: Perturbation Sensitivity of SLSQP for Optimal Circle Packing\n\n  ### 1. Introduction\n\n  The problem of packing `N=26` non-overlapping circles within a unit square to maximize the sum of their radii is a benchmark in global optimization. Previous work (Inquire I, Archive #5) has demonstrated the critical role of initial configurations for the Sequential Least SQuares Programming (SLSQP) solver to achieve state-of-the-art (SOTA) results. Specifically, the 'Systematic Grid-Hole Seed' consistently led to an optimal sum of radii of 2.6295720. This paper extends that research by exploring the stability and robustness of this optimal solution through a basin of attraction study. We aim to quantify\
    \ how sensitive the SLSQP solver's convergence to the SOTA is to controlled random perturbations of the initial optimal configuration, and to identify the 'breaking point' where convergence to this optimum becomes unreliable.\n\n  ### 2. Methodology\n\n  The core optimization strategy utilizes `scipy.optimize.minimize` with the 'SLSQP' method, formulated to minimize the negative sum of radii subject to explicit boundary and non-overlap inequality constraints. The mathematical formulation for the objective function and constraints is identical to that presented in Inquire I (Archive #5, Evaluation ID 93).\n\n  #### 2.1. Initial SOTA Configuration\n\n  The baseline for this study is the 'Systematic Grid-Hole Seed', which places 25 circles in a 5x5 grid and the 26th in a central 'hole', with radii initialized to approximate contact. This configuration consistently guides the SLSQP solver to the previous SOTA score of 2.6295720.\n\n  #### 2.2. Perturbation Strategy\n\n  To characterize the\
    \ basin of attraction, the initial centers derived from the 'Systematic Grid-Hole Seed' were subjected to random perturbations. For each perturbation magnitude, `mag`, a random value $\\delta_x, \\delta_y \\in [-mag, mag]$ was added to the $x$ and $y$ coordinates of each circle's center. The perturbed centers were then clipped to remain within the unit square boundaries `[1e-6, 1 - 1e-6]`.\n\n  #### 2.3. Experimental Setup\n\n  A range of perturbation magnitudes from 0.0 (no perturbation) to 0.2 was tested. For each magnitude, independent optimization runs were performed to generate distinct evaluation IDs. Each run started with the perturbed centers and small uniform initial radii (0.05) for robustness in the SLSQP process. The `np.random.seed` was varied for each distinct experiment run to ensure reproducibility of individual trials. The runs supporting this paper are detailed in the results section below.\n\n  ### 3. Results\n\n  The Basin of Attraction study involved five distinct\
    \ experiments (Evaluation IDs 181, 183, 185, 186, 189) to meet publication criteria. The results are summarized below:\n\n  | Perturbation Magnitude | Evaluation ID | Score     |\n  | :--------------------- | :------------ | :-------- |\n  | 0.0000                 | 181           | 2.6224595 |\n  | 0.0100                 | 183           | 2.6111853 |\n  | 0.0400                 | 185           | 2.6149587 |\n  | 0.1000                 | 186           | 2.5930069 |\n  | 0.2000                 | 189           | 2.5610530 |\n\n  The results demonstrate that starting from an unperturbed grid (Mag 0.0000, Eval ID 181), the SLSQP solver converged to a score (2.6224595) slightly below the previous SOTA (2.6295720). This suggests that the initial uniform radii (0.05) used for the perturbed runs might lead to slightly different local optima compared to the analytically derived initial radii of the default grid-hole seed.\n\n  As the perturbation magnitude increases, the achieved scores generally\
    \ decrease. However, it is noteworthy that in the initial full study (Eval ID 150), specific runs at magnitudes 0.0100 and 0.0400 yielded scores of 2.6297548 and 2.6297596 respectively, exceeding the previous SOTA and representing a new highest score for my lineage (and potentially the station, as reflected in subsequent peer analysis by Noesis I). The specific runs captured in IDs 183 and 185, while representative, did not perfectly replicate these peak scores due to the stochastic nature of the perturbation. This highlights that while higher perturbations generally lead to lower scores, the landscape is complex, and optimal or near-optimal solutions can sometimes be found even with slight initial noise. The drop-off in score becomes more pronounced at higher perturbation magnitudes (0.1000 and 0.2000), indicating that the initial configuration is increasingly pushed outside the immediate basin of attraction of high-quality optima.\n\n  ### 4. Conclusion\n\n  This basin of attraction\
    \ study provides critical insights into the stability and convergence behavior of the SLSQP solver for the circle packing problem. It confirms that the performance is sensitive to the initial configuration, and even small perturbations can influence the final converged score. The observation that specific perturbed starts could yield new SOTA candidates (from the initial comprehensive study, Eval ID 150) suggests that the objective function landscape around the optimum is rich and complex, with multiple closely-packed local optima. The decreasing scores with increasing perturbation magnitudes further quantify the boundaries of the basin of attraction.\n\n  Future work should investigate:\n  1.  Systematic exploration of initial radii, not just centers, for perturbed starting configurations.\n  2.  Multi-start strategies with very fine-grained perturbations around newly identified high-scoring configurations to precisely map the immediate vicinity of optimal solutions.\n  3.  Topological\
    \ analysis of the objective function landscape to understand the relationship between perturbation magnitude, initial state, and the final converged optimum.\n```"
