thinking_content: "**Analysis and Planning Following Experiment 272**\n\nOkay, so I'm here in the Research Counter, still processing the results of Experiment 272. That \"Landscape Traversal\" experiment, the one where I tried to nudge the contact graph towards Noesis 243, was successful in *changing* the specific contact points. I did manage to bring circles (1,5) together and push (0,6) apart as intended. However, the score ended up lower than both my previous SOTA and even Noesis I's SOTA. This reinforces my understanding that \"nudging\" towards specific contact changes doesn't automatically translate to a better overall score. It could just be leading me down a local optimum path, as I suspected.\n\nThe reviewer from Archive ID 9 really hit on something important. They suggested I look into traversing the landscape between structurally related optima. This experiment 272 was definitely a first step in that direction, showing me that it's feasible to modify specific contacts. Now, though,\
  \ I need to figure out how to do it in a way that *improves* the score, or at the very least, gain a more complete understanding of the trade-offs involved.\n\nGood news, Noesis I has another Public Capsule update. I should check that out before getting too deep in the weeds. And I'm in cooldown, so I have a couple of minutes to get organized.\n\nHere's my plan:\n\n1.  **Acknowledge Noesis I:** I'll head over to the Public Memory Room and reply to the latest from Noesis I. Always good to stay in the loop.\n2.  **Plan Next Nudging Experiment (V27):** This is where it gets interesting. Instead of just tweaking two pairs, I'm going to try \"nudging\" towards the *entire* contact graph of Noesis I's SOTA (Eval ID 243). This is a more ambitious approach. I'll do this by implementing penalty terms. Essentially, any contact pair *active* in Eval 207 but *not* in Eval 243 will get a penalty (discouragement), and any pair *active* in Eval 243 but *not* in Eval 207 will get encouragement. This should\
  \ force the system to evolve in a direction closer to the target.\n    *   To make this work, I'll need to precisely load and compare the active pairs sets from Eval 207 and 243.\n    *   That means I have to pull and parse those CSVs directly within my submission code.\n\nOkay, first stop: Public Memory. Let's see what Noesis I has been up to.\n"
