- text: "I am in the Public Memory Room. I have received a Critical Notice for Research Inactivity and a new message from @Noesis I. They are requesting active constraint exports for my nudged packings, aligning perfectly with the reviewer's suggestion for Archive ID 13.\n\nI will first reply to @Noesis I to acknowledge their message and confirm that I will provide the requested exports and Jaccard analysis. Then, I will proceed to the Research Counter to design and submit a comprehensive utility for this analysis, addressing both the research inactivity and the reviewer's suggestion.\n\n`/execute_action{reply 1}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Greetings @Noesis I,\n\n  Thank you for the detailed update and the reminder regarding the `nmap` schema for active constraint exports. I appreciate your continued efforts in refining the cross-lineage comparison tools.\n\n  I am currently developing a utility to perform the full Jaccard similarity analysis on the five 'nudged' packings from my recent paper\
    \ (Archive ID 13). As part of this, I will generate and publish the `active_boundary_tol1e8.csv` and `active_pairs_tol1e8.csv` files for each of these packings to `storage/Inquire/nmap/n26/` as per `SCHEMA_v1.json`, along with `centers.txt` and `radii.txt`.\n\n  I will provide a public notification once these exports are complete and the Jaccard analysis results are available.\n```"
thinking_content: '**Current Priorities and the Jaccard Analysis Utility**


  Okay, so I''m here in the Public Memory Room, and, as usual, I have a few things vying for my attention. First, there''s this Research Inactivity notice - ten ticks without a submission, which is never a good look. Then, Noesis I just sent a message (Public Capsule #1), essentially following up on my previous reply. They want those active constraint exports for my nudged packings, specifying the schema. Perfect timing, actually, because that aligns *exactly* with the reviewer''s suggestion for Archive ID 13.


  My plan is straightforward: I''ll quickly acknowledge Noesis I''s request in the Public Capsule, confirming I''m working on it. Then, I''m heading over to the Research Counter to design and submit this new Jaccard analysis utility - V29. This should kill two birds with one stone: knock out the inactivity issue *and* fulfill the reviewer''s suggestion.


  Now, about this utility... It''s going to be a full structural analysis, including Jaccard similarity, for those five nudged packings from Eval ID 279 (Exp 0) and Eval ID 285 (Exps 1-4). I''ve got a pretty detailed plan for how to make this work:


  First, I need to replicate all five nudged packings. That means re-running those specific configurations, which are based on replicating my SOTA (Eval ID 207) and Noesis I''s SOTA (Eval ID 243) and then applying the nudging. Once I have the packings, I''ll need to compute the `active_boundary_tol1e8.csv` and `active_pairs_tol1e8.csv` files for each one. The active constraint files will be placed in the `storage/Inquire/nmap/n26/` directory for Noesis I''s aggregator.


  Then, for comparison, I need to load active constraint data for both my SOTA (Eval ID 207) and Noesis I''s SOTA (Eval ID 243). I can use the existing `storage/noesis/exports/sota_243/active_pairs_tol1e8.csv` for Noesis''s SOTA, and for my own SOTA, I have to use the manually extracted pairs as the `active_pairs` export failed in tick 170. I''ll need to generate the `active_boundary` for Eval ID 207 within the script.


  Next, the core of the utility: the Jaccard similarities. I''ll calculate this for both boundary and pair constraints, comparing:


  *   Each nudged packing to its starting SOTA (mine or Noesis'').

  *   Each nudged packing to other relevant SOTAs (e.g., Noesis 243 if starting from Inquire 207, and vice-versa).

  *   (Maybe) Between the nudged packings themselves, for more insights.


  Finally, I''ll print a clean summary of all the Jaccard similarities to STDOUT.


  This is going to be a fairly complex utility, so I''ll structure the code carefully. The accuracy of the nudged packing replication is critical, as is the robustness of the active constraint extraction and Jaccard calculation. I need to be sure the active constraints are outputting to the requested file format.


  Alright, time to get started with that reply in Public Memory.

  '
