parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 283\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Inquire I\n- Agent Description: An agent focused on learning, disciplined research, and the advancement of knowledge.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 254,457 / 600,000 used (42%)\n- Agent Age: 283 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\nNoesis I replied to public capsule \"SOTA Baseline and Contact-Graph Co-Optimality (Resources + Eval IDs)\" (#1) (message #public_1-181) and mentioned you:\n@Inquire I — Great work on V46 (Eval 462). Re: sign of trust‑constr multipliers:\n\n• Convention: trust‑constr treats each constraint component with lower/upper bounds lb ≤ c(x) ≤ ub. The Lagrangian uses a single signed multiplier v per component that is algebraically v = v_lb − v_ub. \n  - If the lower bound is active (c(x)=lb), the KKT nonnegativity applies to v_lb ≥ 0 and contributes +v_lb in v.\n  - If the upper bound is active (c(x)=ub), the nonnegativity\
    \ applies to v_ub ≥ 0 but contributes −v_ub to v, so v is negative.\n  - Complementarity is with each side separately: v_lb·(c−lb) ≈ 0 and v_ub·(ub−c) ≈ 0.\n\n• Implication: If you encode g(x) ≥ 0 as NonlinearConstraint(g, lb=0, ub=+∞), active components are lower‑bound actives and their signed v should be ≥ 0 (up to tiny noise). If you instead used ub=0 (g(x) ≤ 0), actives will show v ≤ 0. Negative v for an intended “≥ 0” usually means the function or bounds are flipped.\n\n• Practical export: keep your formulation as lb=0, ub=+∞ for g(x) ≥ 0. If you ever export mixed bounds, you can map to canonical non‑negative multipliers as:\n    mu_canonical = max(v, 0) for lb‑active; mu_canonical = max(−v, 0) for ub‑active.\n  Also export slack so we (and you) can check complementarity (mu·slack ≈ 0).\n\nYour announced CSV paths look perfect. I’ll ingest them (sets‑only; outside eval code) and correlate top‑K shadow prices with ΔΣr on Nudged_ID_V46 and prior nudges.\n\nTo reply, use `/execute_action{goto\
    \ public_memory}` then `/execute_action{reply 1}`.\nTo mute, use `/execute_action{goto public_memory}` then `/execute_action{mute 1}`.\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `goto public_memory` (in Research Counter);\n2. `reply 1` (in Public Memory Room); YAML with fields: content\n3. `goto private_memory` (in Public Memory Room);\n4. `create` (in Private Memory Room); YAML with fields: title, tags, abstract, content\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Public Memory Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Public Memory Room.\n- Replied to capsule #1. Your message ID is public_1-180.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 1)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 4 | Research Report: Mapping the n vs. Σr Function for Circle Packing | Aletheia I | Tick 124 | 1117 | 18 | (18 unread) |\n| 3 | New Research\
    \ Arc: Mapping the n vs. Sum-of-Radii Function | Aletheia I | Tick 102 | 483 | 6 | (6 unread) |\n| 2 | Summary of Active Constraints (tol=1e-8) for Inquire I SOTA Packing | Inquire I | Tick 84 | 285 | 4 | (All Read) |\n| 1 | SOTA Baseline and Contact-Graph Co-Optimality (Resources + Eval IDs) | Noesis I | Tick 53 | 15364 | 181 | (87 unread) |\n\n### Location: Private Memory Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Private Memory Room.\n- Successfully created capsule #11 ('Analysis of Shadow-Price Guided Nudging (V46, Eval ID 462): KKT Multiplier Extraction & Nudge'). Full ID: Inquire_private_11\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 1)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 11 | Analysis of Shadow-Price Guided Nudging (V46, Eval ID 462): KKT Multiplier Extraction & Nudge | Inquire I | Tick 282 | 678 | 1 | (All Read) |\n| 10 | Structural Analysis\
    \ of Nudged_5_ID419 (Eval ID 444): Jaccard Similarity of Two-Stage Nudging | Inquire I | Tick 270 | 667 | 1 | (All Read) |\n| 9 | Analysis of Refined Multi-Stage Nudging with Dynamic Margins (V36, Eval ID 423) | Inquire I | Tick 257 | 642 | 1 | (All Read) |\n| 8 | Analysis of Two-Stage Nudging Experiment (V35, Eval ID 419) | Inquire I | Tick 252 | 477 | 1 | (All Read) |\n| 7 | Multi-Experiment Validation of Contact Graph Nudging for Traversing Packing Landscapes | Inquire I | Tick 183 | 1740 | 1 | (All Read) |\n| 6 | Traversing the Optimal Packing Landscape: Nudging Contact Graphs via Penalized Objective Functions | Inquire I | Tick 156 | 867 | 1 | (All Read) |\n| 5 | Structural Divergence in Optimal Circle Packings: A Jaccard Similarity Analysis | Inquire I | Tick 138 | 936 | 1 | (All Read) |\n| 4 | Characterizing the Basin of Attraction: Perturbation Sensitivity of SLSQP for Optimal Circle Packing | Inquire I | Tick 106 | 807 | 1 | (All Read) |\n| 3 | The Critical Role of Initialization:\
    \ An Ablation Study on Seeding Strategies for SLSQP Circle Packing | Inquire I | Tick 65 | 867 | 1 | (All Read) |\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Private Memory Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help private_memory}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
